WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:21.030
Speaker 1: being interviewed by now, CNN's
Chris Wallace, formerly of Fox News.

00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:33.250
In this clip, Bernie Sanders effectively says
confiscate all income above $999 million per

00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:34.610
year.

00:00:34.610 --> 00:00:37.469
How many people in the United States does
this even apply to?

00:00:37.469 --> 00:00:38.469
It's very few.

00:00:38.469 --> 00:00:40.830
I'll tell you roughly the number in a moment.

00:00:40.830 --> 00:00:49.850
But what we're talking about here is a 100%
effective tax rate on income at $1,000,000,000

00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:51.559
a year or more.

00:00:51.559 --> 00:00:54.399
But don't take my word for it.

00:00:54.399 --> 00:00:55.660
Here's Bernie explaining it himself.

00:00:55.660 --> 00:00:59.480
Speaker 2: So you're saying, sir, billionaire
should not exist?

00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:06.090
So are you basically saying that once you
get to $999 million, that the government should

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:07.700
confiscate all the rest?

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:13.440
I'm saying that we should go back to a very
progressive tax policy like what we had under

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:18.520
Dwight the I's, which would mean that that
over $1,000,000,000, basically it all goes

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:19.520
to the government.

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:22.480
And you may disagree with me, but I'm just
fine.

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:26.880
Yeah, I think people can make it on $999 billion.

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:33.409
Speaker 1: Now, there is some it's not even
debate.

00:01:33.409 --> 00:01:35.659
It's sort of a lack of clarity.

00:01:35.659 --> 00:01:42.930
Is Bernie talking about a 100% marginal tax
rate on all income at a billion or more per

00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:43.930
year?

00:01:43.930 --> 00:01:48.899
Or is Bernie talking about a wealth tax?

00:01:48.899 --> 00:01:54.969
Such that you are always brought back down
to a net worth of $1,000,000,000.

00:01:54.969 --> 00:02:01.659
It's not completely clear, but because he
says the times of Dwight Eisenhower tax policy

00:02:01.659 --> 00:02:06.520
under Eisenhower was a very, very high marginal
tax rate on income.

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:12.220
So for the sake of today's discussion, let's
assume Bernie is talking about income taxes

00:02:12.220 --> 00:02:13.880
rather than a wealth tax.

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:17.630
So the first question is, has this ever been
done before?

00:02:17.630 --> 00:02:18.989
The answer is no.

00:02:18.989 --> 00:02:25.160
There is no historical precedent for a 100%
marginal tax rate, essentially confiscating

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:28.250
all income above a certain threshold.

00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:31.830
There are governments that have put in place
very high tax rates on the wealthy at high

00:02:31.830 --> 00:02:32.880
levels.

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:37.480
That effectively is close to a 100% tax at
an extremely high level.

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:38.900
But we never had 100%.

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:42.840
Now, just because you haven't done something
doesn't mean it's a bad idea.

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:46.890
It's just that it hasn't been done so we don't
have something to look to.

00:02:46.890 --> 00:02:48.890
But that's what really we're talking about
here.

00:02:48.890 --> 00:02:56.090
For the sake of argument, a 100% tax bracket
that starts at $1,000,000,000.

00:02:56.090 --> 00:03:00.099
On your first ten, I forget exactly where
the tax brackets are right now, but on your

00:03:00.099 --> 00:03:04.459
first ten grand, everybody pays zero federally,
of course.

00:03:04.459 --> 00:03:08.480
And then on that next bit, you pay 10%, 15%.

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:14.170
You get all the way up to 35, 37% at about
half a million bucks.

00:03:14.170 --> 00:03:21.500
And then at $1,000,000,000, it's a 100% tax
rate, The highest it ever got in the US, as

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:28.159
Bernie mentions, is under Eisenhower in the
mid 20th century with a roughly 90% marginal

00:03:28.159 --> 00:03:29.189
tax bracket.

00:03:29.189 --> 00:03:32.580
But again, that's on income above a certain
threshold.

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:39.370
There are European countries with very high
tax rates on the wealthy, but it's not 100%.

00:03:39.370 --> 00:03:46.280
Now, what I want to talk about, we're not
going to have a 100% tax rate any time soon.

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:53.920
So what I think is more useful to talk about
is how can we achieve the goal that Bernie

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:55.220
is espousing?

00:03:55.220 --> 00:04:03.739
And the goal here is to actually collect more
in taxes from the very, very, very rich.

00:04:03.739 --> 00:04:08.519
And as we know, there are complex season,
the complexities and difficulties to actually

00:04:08.519 --> 00:04:13.739
enforcing this sort of thing for all of the
different reasons that we've talked about

00:04:13.739 --> 00:04:14.739
it.

00:04:14.739 --> 00:04:18.239
And I will talk to you again now the question
of how many people this applies to is an interesting

00:04:18.239 --> 00:04:19.549
one.

00:04:19.549 --> 00:04:27.620
In 2022, the Forbes billionaire list said
there were 724 billionaires in the US.

00:04:27.620 --> 00:04:34.090
I believe now it's gone up a little bit to
like 735 billionaires in the U.S. They don't

00:04:34.090 --> 00:04:37.840
all earn a billion a year, but some of them
do.

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:44.380
So we're talking about a policy that would
apply to a fraction of the 700 something billionaires

00:04:44.380 --> 00:04:45.760
in the United States.

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:52.220
Now, one of the first issues, as I know many
of you know, is that many of these billionaires

00:04:52.220 --> 00:04:55.940
don't actually earn income.

00:04:55.940 --> 00:04:59.590
They receive dividends and are subject to
capital gains.

00:04:59.590 --> 00:05:04.520
So then it's well, it's not just about income
over 999 billion.

00:05:04.520 --> 00:05:12.790
It's other forms of proceeds as well that
are not actually subject to income tax rates.

00:05:12.790 --> 00:05:18.530
The point here is what are the mechanisms
through which we might achieve a goal that

00:05:18.530 --> 00:05:21.230
is quite frankly, quite popular among the
American people.

00:05:21.230 --> 00:05:28.460
When you survey the American people and you
say, should taxation be used to collect more

00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:33.630
money from the very rich in order to fund
social programs, it's relatively uncontroversial.

00:05:33.630 --> 00:05:34.990
Most Americans say, yes, you should.

00:05:34.990 --> 00:05:39.400
Now, I do a deep dive into this in my clip
called How to Actually Tax the Rich.

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:43.920
There is no one policy that would cover all
situations, but some of the things that could

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:49.270
or should be looked at is okay, raised the
top marginal income tax rate that will cover

00:05:49.270 --> 00:05:52.940
income, earned income.

00:05:52.940 --> 00:05:58.090
But many of these folks don't earn wages and
they're able to reduce actual taxation in

00:05:58.090 --> 00:06:01.860
a lot of different ways, including by receiving
income in other ways.

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:04.970
So then you could look at, well, we could
impose a wealth tax.

00:06:04.970 --> 00:06:10.110
The problem with the wealth tax would be that
these folks would move assets to countries

00:06:10.110 --> 00:06:15.130
with no wealth tax or invest in assets that
are difficult to value, like artwork or rare

00:06:15.130 --> 00:06:16.130
collectibles.

00:06:16.130 --> 00:06:17.130
Okay.

00:06:17.130 --> 00:06:22.940
But again, remember, it's like with gun violence,
no one policy would cover every situation.

00:06:22.940 --> 00:06:27.130
But the idea is to get some of the low hanging
fruit.

00:06:27.130 --> 00:06:30.380
You can close tax loopholes like the carried
interest loophole.

00:06:30.380 --> 00:06:34.699
The counter to that is you can move assets
offshore, you can change business structure.

00:06:34.699 --> 00:06:36.720
You can lobby for a different loophole.

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:40.259
You can shift investments to tax exempt assets
like municipal bonds.

00:06:40.259 --> 00:06:42.880
And it's not realistic for most hedge funds,
certainly.

00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:46.000
But that's another possibility.

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:47.949
You can better fund the IRS.

00:06:47.949 --> 00:06:50.280
US to do more audits.

00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:53.750
Some of the people that we're talking about
are doing everything legally and correctly

00:06:53.750 --> 00:06:56.460
and paying every dollar that they should be
paying in taxes.

00:06:56.460 --> 00:06:58.990
So audits wouldn't recover any money from
them.

00:06:58.990 --> 00:07:01.300
But there are some that that that are not.

00:07:01.300 --> 00:07:09.300
And so increasing IRS resources is an idea
a financial transaction tax with the ultra

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:15.930
wealthy engaging in certain types of transactions
that average income people do not engage in.

00:07:15.930 --> 00:07:18.160
A financial transaction tax is a possibility.

00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:22.780
Of course, the way to avoid that would be
Wait, just wait.

00:07:22.780 --> 00:07:27.090
Move assets to countries that don't have such
a tax, etc.

00:07:27.090 --> 00:07:31.479
You could require country by country income
reporting for companies.

00:07:31.479 --> 00:07:37.720
One of the ways that companies can avoid corporate
tax is now we're talking about companies rather

00:07:37.720 --> 00:07:44.930
than individuals is shifting income to other
countries that is no longer subject to American

00:07:44.930 --> 00:07:45.930
taxation.

00:07:45.930 --> 00:07:50.040
You could pass a law that says no, that has
to be reported and there has to be some kind

00:07:50.040 --> 00:07:52.830
of corporate minimum tax that is paid.

00:07:52.830 --> 00:07:56.020
You can also look at raising the tax rate
on capital gains.

00:07:56.020 --> 00:08:00.860
And so that would get around this issue of
a lot of these very rich folks aren't earning

00:08:00.860 --> 00:08:04.050
income subject to income taxes.

00:08:04.050 --> 00:08:06.490
So you can raise the tax on capital gains
the way around.

00:08:06.490 --> 00:08:10.889
That would be you just wait to realize the
gains or look at other mechanisms.

00:08:10.889 --> 00:08:14.720
And you can also expand the alternative minimum
tax.

00:08:14.720 --> 00:08:20.960
We already know that with a minimum tax, high
earners can still reduce their taxable income

00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:24.290
through deductions, tax credits and other
strategies.

00:08:24.290 --> 00:08:28.610
But increasing that minimum tax is another
approach.

00:08:28.610 --> 00:08:31.740
So those are ideas to achieve.

00:08:31.740 --> 00:08:35.690
What is the underlying principle that Bernie
is espousing here?

00:08:35.690 --> 00:08:41.080
I don't expect a 100% tax bracket any time
soon in the United States.

00:08:41.080 --> 00:09:57.090
I never say never, but certainly not in at
least the next five years.

